```json
{"blocks": [
{"id": "r1", "winner": "A", "why": "Draft A delivers a complete, chaptered novel with a finished ending and layered mysteries (Bai Li's identity, the Shadow, the sealed entity), while Draft B is only an opening segment that stops before the story is complete."},
{"id": "r2", "winner": "A", "why": "Draft A keeps the pressure up across chapters with cliffhanger endings such as '那不是幻觉' and '倒下之前……', whereas Draft B has tense prose but only one scene with a single hook."},
{"id": "r3", "winner": "B", "why": "Draft B weaves Lin Ye's fear, hesitation, and worry for his mother into the mine fight and the monster battle, so the inner state and the action drive each other, while Draft A's combat is mostly detached from its characters' inner conflicts."},
{"id": "r4", "winner": "A", "why": "Draft A covers every required plot node in order (miner, medicine for mother, forbidden zone, blood jade, bloodline awakening, monsters, Bai Li's reveal of Lingshu, multi-faction plot, and stopping the emperor at the Tiantong Tower), while Draft B stops after the monster outbreak."},
{"id": "r5", "winner": "A", "why": "Draft A gives the emperor a grey, sympathetic backstory of suffering that leads him to a corrupt choice, has Bai Li and Lin Ye make costly sacrifices, and explicitly states in the climax that the value of Lingshu is balance and protection."},
{"id": "r6", "winner": "A", "why": "Draft A does pay off its tower foreshadowing (the sealed sphere, chains, and stones), but it has real inconsistencies (the key-stone count shifts from seven to six projections, the Shadow's 'devouring the previous era's evil god' contradicts the seal's origin, and Bing Yue's stone appears with no setup), whereas Draft B is too short to reach the payoff at all."}
]}
```

